{
  "dn4:0.1": "Long Discourses 4 ",
  "dn4:0.2": "With Soṇadaṇḍa ",
  "dn4:1.0": "1. The Brahmins and Householders of Campā ",
  "dn4:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "dn4:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Aṅgas together with a large Saṅgha of five hundred mendicants when he arrived at Campā, ",
  "dn4:1.3": "where he stayed by the banks of the Gaggarā Lotus Pond. ",
  "dn4:1.4": "Now at that time the brahmin Soṇadaṇḍa was living in Campā. It was a crown property given by King Seniya Bimbisāra of Magadha, teeming with living creatures, full of hay, wood, water, and grain, a royal park endowed to a brahmin. ",
  "dn4:2.1": "The brahmins and householders of Campā heard: ",
  "dn4:2.2": "“It seems the ascetic Gotama—a Sakyan, gone forth from a Sakyan family—has arrived at Campā and is staying on the banks of the Gaggarā Lotus Pond. ",
  "dn4:2.3": "He has this good reputation: ",
  "dn4:2.4": "‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ ",
  "dn4:2.5": "He has realized with his own insight this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—and he makes it known to others. ",
  "dn4:2.6": "He teaches Dhamma that’s good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased. And he reveals a 